playwright is available in the kernel. So the real browser verification is possible. Plan:

1. Serve the repo over HTTP (ES modules need http, not file://). Use Node's http server in a small script — or better, use the repo's node to serve `D:/dev/ab-tasks/chessground-322-qwen-med-r1` on a port. Can do it in the kernel itself (node:http is available; "node: builtins are generally available via dynamic import"). But the kernel's child process — can it bind ports? Probably yes. Simpler and more robust is: spin up a small static server in a background bash process (node one-liner... but "inline scripts" are discouraged in bash — "inline scripts, heredocs, $(…), complex control flow → purpose-built tool or checked-in script". A one-line `node -e` static server is borderline. Alternative: write a small throwaway server file? I don't want to add a file to the repo for this... could write to tmpDir (nodeRepl.tmpDir) and run from bash.

Actually, the simplest, cleanest approach: do it all inside the kernel:
- Import node:http, serve the repo directory (static files with content types) on 127.0.0.1:0 (random port).
- Use playwright chromium to open http://127.0.0.1:PORT/demo.html.
- Collect console errors + pageerrors.
- Wait for boards to render: e.g. wait for `#board-1 cg-board` — hmm, the board element is `cg-board` (custom tag, created by createEl? createEl makes an element with the tagName 'cg-board'? createEl in util.ts — creates an HTMLElement with tagName? Let me recall: chessground uses custom tags like `cg-board`, `piece`, `square` — created via document.createElement('cg-board') (unknown custom elements work in the browser). So selector: `#board-1 cg-board square` — 64 square elements; `#board-1 piece` — 32 pieces.
- Assert: board-1 has 64 squares + 32 pieces; board-2 (fen) has 64 squares + pieces for that fen (let me count: 'r2q2k1/1p6/p2p4/2pN1rp1/N1Pb2Q1/8/PP1B4/R6K b - - 2 25' — pieces: r,q,k (3) + p (1) + p,p (2) + p,N,r,p (4) + N,p,b,Q (4) + P,P,B,K (4) + R,K (2) = 3+1+2+4+4+4+2 = 20 pieces); board-3 has svg shapes (autoShapes 7 → 7 paths or so in `.cg-shapes`); board-4 orientation is black.
- Screenshot too? Could emit an image via nodeRepl.emitImage — nice evidence. Let me take a screenshot of the page.
- Then close browser and server.

Is chromium installed for playwright? `playwright: available` means the package is resolvable, but the browser binary might not be installed. Let me try `chromium.launch()` — if binary missing, the error will say so. Check playwright-core availability — the browser might be installed system-wide. Let's try.

Timeout: give it 60s.

Server: static file server with content types: .html → text/html, .js → text/javascript, .css → text/css, .json → application/json, default application/octet-stream. Serve from the repo root. Handle URL path → file, prevent path traversal (basic).

Let me write the kernel code. Watch out for top-level bindings: use `var` for anything I might reassign; unique names.

Code:

```js
var demoHttp = (await import('node:http'));
var demoFs = await import('node:fs/promises');
var demoPath = await import('node:path');
const root = nodeRepl.cwd;
const types = { '.html': 'text/html', '.js': 'text/javascript', '.css': 'text/css', '.json': 'application/json' };
var demoServer = demoHttp.default.createServer(async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const url = new URL(req.url, 'http://x');
    let p = decodeURIComponent(url.pathname);
    if (p === '/') p = '/demo.html';
    const file = demoPath.default.resolve(root, '.' + p);
    if (!file.startsWith(root)) { res.writeHead(403); res.end(); return; }
    const data = await demoFs.readFile(file);
    res.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': types[demoPath.default.extname(file)] || 'application/octet-stream' });
    res.end(data);
  } catch (e) { res.writeHead(404); res.end('not found'); }
});
await new Promise(r => demoServer.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', r));
var demoPort = demoServer.address().port;
nodeRepl.write('serving on ' + demoPort);
```

Wait — can the kernel import node:http? The docs say "node: builtins are generally available via dynamic import, but the model code cannot import process". http should be fine.

Then, in a second call (or the same one), the playwright part:

```js
var pw = await import('playwright');
var demoBrowser = await pw.chromium.launch();
var demoPage = await demoBrowser.newPage({ viewport: { width: 1400, height: 900 } });
var demoErrors = [];
demoPage.on('console', m => { if (m.type() === 'error') demoErrors.push(m.text()); });
demoPage.on('pageerror', e => demoErrors.push(String(e)));
await demoPage.goto('http://127.0.0.1:' + demoPort + '/demo.html', { waitUntil: 'networkidle' });
// wait for all 5 boards to render
await demoPage.waitForSelector('#board-5 cg-board square', { timeout: 10000 });
const stats = await demoPage.evaluate(() => {
  const out = [];
  for (const id of ['board-1','board-2','board-3','board-4','board-5']) {
    const el = document.getElementById(id);
    out.push({ id, squares: el.querySelectorAll('square').length, pieces: el.querySelectorAll('piece').length, shapes: el.querySelectorAll('.cg-shapes path').length });
  }
  return out;
});
var demoShot = await demoPage.screenshot();
await demoBrowser.close();
demoServer.close();
nodeRepl.write({ demoErrors, stats });
await nodeRepl.emitImage(demoShot);
```

Hmm, `square`/`piece` are custom elements — querySelectorAll('square') matches by tag name, works in browser.

Note: `waitForSelector('#board-5 cg-board square')` — the board renders synchronously during the Chessground() call; the script is in the head and runs before body is parsed... wait, the script is a module in <head> — module scripts are deferred by default, so they run after DOM parsing → getElementById('board-1') works. Rendering is synchronous (redrawNow) — squares exist right away. But there's a subtlety: the script runs after parsing (deferred), but the body elements exist. Good.

networkidle with the local server is fine. Or 'load'.

One risk: playwright chromium binary not installed → launch fails with an executable path error. Fallback in that case: `pw.chromium.launch({ executablePath })`? Or `pw.chromium.launch({ channel: 'chrome' })` to use system Chrome — per workstation, NVIDIA RTX 4090 Windows machine, Tabby terminal — Chrome/Edge is probably installed. Handle in code: try default launch, catch → try channel 'chrome', catch → try channel 'msedge'.

Let me do it in 2 calls: (1) start the server; (2) browser + assertions + screenshot; (3) cleanup. Actually (1)+(2) in one call is fine, cleanup in a follow-up call on failure... no — keep server and browser in top-level `var` bindings so a later call can clean up even if the assertion throws. Put the whole flow in one call, but if it throws mid-way, the server/browser stay open (bindings persist) and the next call can clean up.

Let me combine the server + browser into one call with generous timeout (60000).

